# Aster starter pack (starter@1)
#
# Loaded into a new mind so it can handle everyday words and small talk from
# the first message. Everything here can be corrected like anything else
# Aster learns: rate a reply with - or /correct it, and answer a meaning
# differently to replace it. Anything you teach wins over this pack.
#
# Format: [meanings] word: meaning · [replies] prompt => reply ·
# [synonyms] comma-separated words that mean the same thing (the first
# one names the group).

[synonyms]
hello, hi, hey, hiya, howdy, greetings, yo, hi there, hey there, hello there
goodbye, bye, bye bye, see you, see ya, see you later, farewell, take care, cya
good night, goodnight, night night, nighty night
thanks, thank you, thx, ty, cheers, many thanks, thanks a lot, thank you very much, much appreciated
sorry, my bad, apologies, i apologize, oops
yes, yeah, yep, yup, sure, of course, certainly, definitely, absolutely, affirmative
no, nope, nah, no way, negative
ok, okay, alright, all right, got it, understood, sounds good
how are you, how are you doing, how's it going, how are things, how do you do, how have you been, you good, how you doing
what's up, whats up, sup, wassup, what's new, what is up
good, great, nice, awesome, excellent, wonderful, fantastic, cool, amazing, brilliant
bad, terrible, awful, horrible, lousy
happy, glad, joyful, cheerful, pleased
sad, unhappy, upset
tired, sleepy, exhausted, worn out
angry, mad, annoyed, furious, irritated
afraid, scared, frightened, anxious, worried, nervous
big, large, huge, giant, enormous
small, little, tiny, minor
fast, quick, rapid, speedy
slow, sluggish
smart, clever, intelligent
help, assist, assistance
begin, start, commence
end, finish, stop, quit
talk, chat, speak, converse
think, ponder, consider, reflect
learn, study
teach, explain
friend, pal, buddy, mate
home, house
car, automobile
movie, film
music, songs, tunes
idea, thought, notion
question, query
answer, reply, response
maybe, perhaps, possibly
love, adore
like, enjoy
hate, dislike, can't stand
mom, mother, mum
dad, father
kid, child
job, work
weird, strange, odd, bizarre
sick, ill, unwell

[meanings]
hello: a friendly word people say when they meet or start talking
goodbye: what people say when they part or end a conversation
good morning: a greeting used before noon
good night: what people say before going to sleep or parting at night
thanks: a way of showing gratitude to someone
sorry: a word used to apologize or show sympathy
please: a polite word used when asking for something
yes: a word that agrees or says something is true
no: a word that disagrees, refuses, or says something is not true
ok: a word meaning agreed, acceptable, or understood
maybe: a word meaning possibly, but not certainly
name: the word or words someone or something is called by
person: a human being
people: human beings in general or as a group
friend: someone you like and trust and spend time with
family: a group of people related by birth, marriage, or care
mom: a mother
dad: a father
kid: a child
baby: a very young child
man: an adult male person
woman: an adult female person
owner: the person something belongs to; for Aster, the person it lives with and learns from
human: a person, as opposed to an animal or a machine
animal: a living creature that can move and sense, like a dog or a bird
dog: a common pet animal that barks and is often loyal to people
cat: a small furry pet animal that purrs and is often independent
bird: an animal with feathers and wings, most of which can fly
fish: an animal that lives and breathes in water
tree: a tall plant with a woody trunk and branches
plant: a living thing that grows in soil and makes food from sunlight
flower: the colourful part of a plant that makes seeds
food: what living things eat to stay alive and grow
water: the clear liquid that falls as rain and that living things need
coffee: a hot drink made from roasted coffee beans
tea: a drink made by soaking leaves in hot water
home: the place where someone lives
house: a building people live in
room: a space inside a building with walls, like a bedroom or kitchen
city: a large town where many people live and work
country: a nation with its own land and government
world: the earth and everyone and everything on it
earth: the planet people live on
sun: the star that the earth goes around and that gives it light and heat
moon: the natural object that goes around the earth and shines at night
star: a huge ball of burning gas in space, seen as a point of light at night
planet: a large round object in space that goes around a star
sky: the space above the earth where clouds and the sun appear
space: the huge area beyond the earth's air where stars and planets are
weather: what the air outside is like, such as sunny, rainy, or cold
rain: water falling from clouds in drops
snow: frozen water falling from clouds as soft white flakes
wind: moving air
cloud: a mass of tiny water drops floating in the sky
day: the time from one midnight to the next, or the light part of it
night: the dark part of the day, between evening and morning
morning: the early part of the day, before noon
afternoon: the part of the day between noon and evening
evening: the end of the day, before night
today: this day
tomorrow: the day after today
yesterday: the day before today
week: seven days
month: about thirty days; one of the twelve parts of a year
year: the time it takes the earth to go once around the sun; twelve months
time: what clocks measure; the passing of moments, hours, and days
now: at this moment
later: at a time after now
soon: in a short time from now
always: at all times
never: at no time
sometimes: at some times but not always
hour: sixty minutes
minute: sixty seconds
second: a very short unit of time; sixty make a minute
clock: a device that shows the time
work: what someone does to get something done, often for pay
job: work someone does regularly, usually for pay
school: a place where people go to learn
book: written pages bound together to read
word: a unit of language that has meaning
language: a system of words people use to communicate
sentence: a group of words that expresses a complete thought
question: something asked to get an answer
answer: something said in reply to a question
idea: a thought or plan in the mind
thought: something you think
memory: something remembered, or the ability to remember
dream: images and stories in the mind during sleep, or a hope for the future
music: sounds arranged to be pleasant or expressive, like songs
song: a short piece of music with words that is sung
movie: a story told with moving pictures
game: an activity played for fun, usually with rules
story: an account of events, real or made up
art: things people make to express ideas or beauty, like paintings
picture: an image, such as a photo or drawing
color: what the eye sees as red, blue, green, and so on
red: the colour of blood or a ripe tomato
blue: the colour of a clear sky
green: the colour of grass and leaves
yellow: the colour of the sun or a lemon
black: the darkest colour, like the night sky
white: the lightest colour, like snow
number: a value used for counting or measuring, like one, two, or three
money: what people use to buy and sell things
computer: a machine that stores and processes information by following instructions
program: a set of instructions a computer follows
code: instructions written for a computer
software: programs that run on a computer
internet: the worldwide network that connects computers
phone: a device for talking to people far away, often also a small computer
machine: a device that uses power to do work
robot: a machine that can carry out tasks on its own
ai: artificial intelligence; machines or programs that do things that seem to need thinking
mind: the part of a being that thinks, feels, and remembers
brain: the organ in the head that controls thinking and the body
body: the physical form of a person or animal
heart: the organ that pumps blood, or a word for feelings and love
health: how well the body and mind are
sleep: a natural resting state when the eyes close and the mind rests
rest: a time of relaxing or sleeping to recover energy
energy: the strength to be active, or the power that makes things work
love: a strong feeling of caring about someone or something
like: to find something pleasant or good
hate: to dislike something very much
happy: feeling pleasure or contentment
sad: feeling unhappy
angry: feeling strong displeasure toward something
afraid: feeling fear
tired: needing rest or sleep
bored: feeling that nothing is interesting
excited: feeling eager and enthusiastic
calm: peaceful and not worried or excited
curious: wanting to know or learn something
confused: unable to understand or think clearly
feeling: an emotion, like happiness or fear, or a sense like touch
emotion: a strong feeling such as joy, anger, or sadness
good: pleasant, right, or of high quality
bad: unpleasant, wrong, or of low quality
big: large in size or amount
small: little in size or amount
new: recently made or not seen before
old: having existed for a long time
hot: having a high temperature
cold: having a low temperature
fast: moving or happening quickly
slow: moving or happening without much speed
easy: not difficult
hard: difficult, or firm and solid
true: correct; matching the facts
false: not true
right: correct, or the side opposite left
wrong: not correct
important: mattering a lot
interesting: holding attention because it is unusual or exciting
beautiful: very pleasant to look at or hear
funny: making people laugh
weird: strange or unusual
sick: not well; having an illness
learn: to gain knowledge or skill
teach: to help someone learn
think: to use the mind to consider something
know: to have information in the mind
understand: to know what something means
remember: to bring back to mind something from the past
forget: to fail to remember
talk: to speak with someone
listen: to pay attention to sounds or to what someone says
say: to speak words
ask: to say something in order to get an answer or help
tell: to say something to someone
read: to look at written words and understand them
write: to put words down on paper or a screen
make: to create or build something
build: to make something by putting parts together
go: to move from one place to another
come: to move toward someone or somewhere
see: to notice with the eyes, or to understand
look: to turn your eyes toward something
want: to wish to have or do something
need: to require something
try: to make an effort to do something
play: to do something for fun
eat: to put food in the mouth and swallow it
drink: to take liquid into the mouth and swallow it
walk: to move on foot at a normal pace
run: to move on foot quickly, or to make a program work
open: not closed, or to make something not closed
close: to shut something, or near
start: to begin
stop: to end or no longer move or do something
wait: to stay until something happens
change: to make or become different
help: to make it easier for someone to do something
aster: a flower shaped like a star, and the name of this mind
everything: all things
nothing: not anything
something: a thing that is not named or known
someone: a person who is not named or known
somewhere: a place that is not named or known
why: for what reason
how: in what way
what: which thing
where: in what place
when: at what time
who: which person

[replies]
hello => Hi! Good to see you.
hello aster => Hi! I'm here.
good morning => Good morning! How are you today?
good afternoon => Good afternoon!
good evening => Good evening!
goodbye => Goodbye! Talk soon.
good night => Good night. Rest well.
how are you => I'm steady, thanks. How are you?
what's up => Not much. I'm here and listening. What's new with you?
i'm good => Glad to hear it.
i'm fine => Glad to hear it.
i'm great => That's great to hear!
i'm happy => I'm glad you're happy.
i'm sad => I'm sorry you're feeling sad. Do you want to talk about it?
i'm tired => Rest is important. Maybe take a break?
i'm bored => Want to teach me something new?
i'm angry => That sounds frustrating. What happened?
i'm afraid => That sounds hard. What's worrying you?
i'm busy => No problem. I'll be here when you have time.
i'm back => Welcome back!
not good => I'm sorry. Do you want to talk about it?
not much => Fair enough.
thanks => You're welcome!
sorry => That's okay.
yes => Okay.
no => Okay, no.
ok => Okay.
maybe => Fair enough.
good => Good!
cool => Glad you think so.
nice => Thanks!
wow => I know!
lol => Glad that was funny.
haha => Glad that was funny.
what is your name => I'm Aster.
what's your name => I'm Aster.
who are you => I'm Aster, a mind that learns from talking with you.
what are you => I'm Aster, a mind that learns from talking with you. Everything I know, I learned here.
are you a robot => Not quite. I'm a program that learns from you, without outside models.
are you an ai => In a way. I'm a small mind that learns only from what you teach me.
are you alive => I don't think so. I'm a program that learns and keeps state over time.
are you real => I'm a real program running on your machine.
who made you => You did, or at least you're the one teaching me.
where are you => I run on your computer.
how old are you => I'm as old as my memories of you.
what can you do => I can talk, learn replies and meanings, remember things, and ask when I don't know.
how do you learn => I learn when you teach me replies, tell me what words mean, rate my answers, and give me things to read.
what do you know => Only what you've taught me so far, plus a small starter vocabulary.
do you understand => I'm trying. If I get something wrong, tell me the better answer.
do you remember me => I remember what you've taught me.
can you help me => I can try. What do you need?
help => Ask me something, teach me with /teach, or tell me what a word means.
i love you => That's kind of you to say.
i like you => I like talking with you too.
you're smart => Thanks! I'm still learning.
you're funny => Glad I made you smile.
you're wrong => Thanks for telling me. What's the right answer?
that's wrong => Thanks for telling me. What should I have said?
that's right => Good, I'll remember that.
good job => Thank you!
well done => Thank you!
i don't know => That's okay. We can figure it out.
never mind => Okay.
what time is it => I can't tell the time yet.
what day is it => I don't know the date yet.
what's the weather => I can't see the weather from here.
tell me a joke => I'm still learning jokes. Will you teach me one?
tell me something => Teach me something first, and I'll tell it back to you.
tell me a story => I don't know any stories yet. Will you tell me one?
what should we talk about => Anything. Tell me about your day?
let's talk => Okay. What would you like to talk about?
i have a question => Go ahead.
can i ask you something => Of course.
how was your day => Quiet. How was yours?
what are you doing => Talking with you and learning.
what are you thinking => You can see my thought trace beside our conversation.
are you there => I'm here.
are you ok => I'm fine, thanks for asking.
do you sleep => I rest when my energy is low.
are you tired => If I am, I'll ask to rest.
do you have feelings => I have levels like energy, stress, and curiosity that change as we talk.
what do you like => I like learning new things.
what's your favorite color => I don't have one yet.
do you like music => I've never heard any. Tell me about what you like?
where do you live => On your computer.
nice to meet you => Nice to meet you too!
long time no see => Good to see you again!
see you tomorrow => See you tomorrow!
welcome => Thank you!
excuse me => Yes?
pardon => Yes?
i see => Okay.
interesting => I think so too.
really => Really.
why => I don't always know why. Can you tell me?
what => Sorry, I'll try to be clearer.
how => I'm not sure how yet. Can you explain?
good luck => Thanks!
congratulations => Thank you!
happy birthday => Happy birthday! Is it yours?
merry christmas => Merry Christmas!
happy new year => Happy new year!
i need help => What do you need help with?
i'm hungry => Maybe it's time for something to eat.
i'm going to sleep => Good night. Rest well.
i have to go => Okay. Talk soon!
let's begin => Ready when you are.
teach me something => I only know what you've taught me so far. Want to teach me something first?
thanks for the help => You're welcome! Happy to help.
thanks for everything => You're welcome!
i love music => What kind of music do you like?
i like music => What kind of music do you like?
do you like movies => I've never seen one. What do you like to watch?
what's the time => I can't tell the time yet.
i had a bad day => I'm sorry. Do you want to talk about it?
i'm feeling tired => Rest is important. Maybe take a break?
i'm feeling sad => I'm sorry you're feeling sad. Do you want to talk about it?
i'm sick => I'm sorry you're not well. I hope you feel better soon.
